WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.069 --> 00:00:05.900
With The Flash back on TV, let's take a look
at 10 things you probably didn't know about

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:09.389
the Scarlet Speedster's show.

00:00:09.389 --> 00:00:14.429
According to the show's casting director David
Rapaport, before Grant Gustin was cast as

00:00:14.429 --> 00:00:18.720
Barry Allen on The Flash, he auditioned to
play Thea Queen's boyfriend in the first season

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:19.869
of Arrow!

00:00:19.869 --> 00:00:24.670
And Andy Mientus who brings to life the villainous
Pied Piper in The Flash initially read for

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:26.310
the role of the Scarlet Speedster.

00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:31.570
Oh, and by the way, Robbie Amell, who plays
Ronnie Raymond, one half of Firestorm, originally

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:37.250
auditioned for Oliver Queen on Arrow, though,
of course, that role went to Stephen Amell,

00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:39.550
Robbie's cousin!

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:44.719
Storyboard artist Adrien van Viersen made
over 400 boards for The Flash pilot, compared

00:00:44.719 --> 00:00:47.629
to only half that number for the Arrow pilot.

00:00:47.629 --> 00:00:52.219
According to van Viersen, The Flash is the
biggest pilot he and director David Nutter

00:00:52.219 --> 00:00:56.710
have ever worked on together, and bear in
mind they've made pilots for shows including

00:00:56.710 --> 00:01:01.030
Arrow, Smallville, and Terminator: The Sarah
Connor Chronicles.

00:01:01.030 --> 00:01:06.280
The Flash is particularly complex as the stunt
and visual effects teams have to combine the

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:10.490
“speed force” effects with the live action,
and the storyboards are especially useful

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:11.610
for this.

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:17.530
About one-sixth of The Flash is computer animated,
and in the pilot there were about 200 visual

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:19.920
effects.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:24.579
Production designer Tyler Harron based the
look of the show's particle accelerator set

00:01:24.579 --> 00:01:29.680
on the Large Hadron Collider, though the set
is obviously a lot smaller at just 40 foot

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:30.850
by 20 foot.

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:33.700
Also, the treadmill that Barry Allen runs
on at S.T.A.R.

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:38.850
Labs was actually built from scratch for the
show and can go up to 16 miles an hour!

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:43.360
In an interview at PaleyFest, Grant Gustin
said that, at some point in the future, the

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:44.360
S.T.A.R.

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:49.189
Labs treadmill will become the Cosmic Treadmill
which, in the DC comics, Barry used to travel

00:01:49.189 --> 00:01:51.780
through time.

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:55.020
If you've ever wondered just what the Metahumans
locked up under S.T.A.R.

00:01:55.020 --> 00:02:00.920
Labs ate, a deleted scene from Season One
episode 21, Grodd Lives, revealed the answer!

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:06.170
According to Caitlin Snow, The Weather Wizard
is a Thai food fiend, Peak-a-Boo just loves

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:09.290
enchiladas, and Deathbolt is a vegan.

00:02:09.290 --> 00:02:13.500
And Dr Snow didn't say who they were but,
judging by her food trolley, there were quite

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:17.770
a few Big Belly Burger fans down there too!

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:21.910
Speaking of Big Belly Burger, the fictional
fast food chain, which appears in both The

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:28.860
Flash and Arrow TV shows, first appeared in
DC Comics back in June 1988 in issue 441 of

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:30.300
The Adventures of Superman.

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:35.110
The Big Belly Burger of the comic books was
founded in Coast City, is a subsidiary of

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:42.480
Lex Luthor's LexCorp, and was actually inspired
by the real-life Big Boy restaurant chain.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:47.340
Composer Blake Neely, who scores both The
Flash and Arrow, included a special musical

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:51.040
Easter Egg in Tricksters, episode 17 of the
first season.

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:55.840
In the episode, Mark Hamill, who appeared
in the original live-action TV series of The

00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:01.390
Flash in the early 1990s, returned as the
villainous James Jesse aka The Trickster.

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:06.440
So, Neely used composer Shirley Walker's Trickster
theme which she wrote and conducted for the

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:08.010
1990s show.

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:12.180
And Hamill isn't the only actor from that
early Flash show to make a comeback.

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:17.060
Vito D'Ambrosio, who played police officer
Anthony Bellows in the original show returned

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:21.720
as the same character for seasons 1 and 2
of the new show, though this time he's Mayor

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:23.590
of Central City.

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:27.670
Amanda Pays also returned as scientist Dr
Tina McGee.

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:32.440
And John Wesley Shipp, who plays Barry Allen's
father, actually starred as the Scarlet Speedster

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:34.610
in the original series.

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:41.220
Actor Jesse L Martin's character Detective
Joe West doesn't actually exist in the comics.

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:46.940
In the comics, Iris's father is either the
scientist Ira West or the alcoholic William

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:47.940
West.

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:52.550
But despite the fact Joe West was created
especially for the TV series, he almost didn't

00:03:52.550 --> 00:03:56.680
even make it to the second episode as the
creators were considering killing him off

00:03:56.680 --> 00:03:57.680
in the pilot!

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:02.590
And if you've ever wondered why Joe is often
seen wearing a black beanie, it was Jesse

00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:08.390
L. Martin's idea, partly to give Joe a bit
of a different look to NYPD Detective Ed Green,

00:04:08.390 --> 00:04:12.320
who Martin played on TV's Law &amp; Order for
nearly a decade.

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:16.350
If you've seen the musical Rent though, the
beanie may remind you of Martin's character

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:17.730
in that.

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:23.400
Grodd's cage very nearly didn't make it into
the show's pilot due to the cost of building

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:24.400
it.

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:28.480
Later when Grodd himself appeared on screen,
unlike Peter Jackson's King Kong or the recent

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:33.020
Planet Of The Apes movies, the show did not
use performance capture to bring its sizeable

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:35.110
simian star to life.

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:40.770
Instead, visual effects supervisor Armen Kevorkian
and his crew at Encore VFX built Grodd from

00:04:40.770 --> 00:04:46.120
scratch on the computer, creating his skeleton,
then adding muscles, and then skin and fur.

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:51.610
The show's exec producers first gave Kevorkian
the heads-up, soon after finishing the pilot,

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:53.950
that Grodd was going to feature in season
one.

00:04:53.950 --> 00:04:58.310
For inspiration on Grodd's design, the visual
effects crew looked at the comic books for

00:04:58.310 --> 00:05:01.960
his size and attitude, and at videos of real
gorillas too.

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:06.920
Kevorkian also roughly acted out Grodd's moves
and facial expressions for the animators,

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:11.720
who sometimes also recorded themselves to
check what did and didn't work.

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:16.890
The presence on set of a stunt actor in a
large Grodd suit and stilts provided further

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:21.570
inspiration for the gorilla's actions and
also helped the crew frame each shot.

00:05:21.570 --> 00:05:27.220
Just as the Flash's speed has increased over
the course of the series, the rate at which

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:29.360
the show is shot has also sped up.

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:34.270
For example, in the early days, they'd need
20 to 30 takes to film a straightforward shot

00:05:34.270 --> 00:05:35.270
of the Flash running.

00:05:35.270 --> 00:05:39.530
But now, they often get what they need in
just a couple of takes.

00:05:39.530 --> 00:05:45.020
When Grant Gustin first started putting on
his Flash costume, it took him a whole 40

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:48.910
minutes to suit up, mainly because his mask
had to be glued to his face.

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:54.340
However, after episode eight of season one,
Gustin got a new mask that looks the same

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:58.710
but slides on with a zipper, which means it
now only takes him a speedy 10 to 15 minutes

00:05:58.710 --> 00:06:00.880
to slip into his superhero suit.

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:04.980
The original costume, which is mostly made
of leather with some added stretchy material

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:07.980
for ease of movement, was designed by Colleen
Atwood.

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:13.140
Maya Mani, who designed Arrow's season 4 suit,
is behind the changes to The Flash's season

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:17.760
2 costume, which harks back to the comic books
with its emblem featuring a lightning bolt

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:19.740
on a white rather than red background.

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:24.160
Now, let me know in the comments below, who
are your favourite characters on The Flash?

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:27.930
And what other characters from the comics
would you like to see on the show?

00:06:27.930 --> 00:06:31.720
If you enjoyed this video, do please share
it, hit the thumbs-up button and subscribe

00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:35.300
for more things you didn't know, as well as
movie reviews and interviews.

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:36.820
Thanks for watching!

00:06:36.820 --> 00:06:38.060
Yippee-ki-yay, TV lovers!

